"""Individually authored audience/purpose tasks with self-contained textual evidence."""
import json, re
from pathlib import Path
rows=[]
def q(text,ask,correct,w1,w2,w3,reason):rows.append((text,ask,correct,[w1,w2,w3],reason))
q('Notice: Families, the school concert begins at 6 pm. Please collect your child from their classroom afterwards.','Who especially needs the collection instruction?','The children’s parents or carers.','People repairing the stage lights.','A book’s imaginary characters.','Visitors who have no child performing.','Collect your child directly addresses the adults responsible for the performing children.')
q('Label: This model shows a bridge with two towers. The cables support the road between them.','What is the label mainly doing?','Explaining parts of the model.','Urging visitors to buy the bridge.','Telling an adventure about two towers.','Giving directions to the nearest road.','It names parts and explains how they relate; there is no sales request or story event.')
q('Instructions: Players, take three cards each. Put the remaining cards face down. The youngest player begins.','Which audience is addressed by these instructions?','People about to play the card game.','People buying a new table.','Readers looking for a bedtime story.','Parents collecting children after a concert.','Players, cards and begins provide practical information for taking part in this game.')
q('Report: Our class planted six beans. Four shoots appeared by Friday. Two pots had no shoots yet.','Which purpose is best supported by the numbers and observations?','To report what the class observed.','To persuade every class to plant beans.','To entertain with a talking-bean story.','To sell pots to gardeners.','The writer records observed counts and timing without a request or imaginary action.')
q('Guide: If you are visiting our library for the first time, return books through the slot beside the desk.','Why does this suit new library visitors?','It explains where to do something they may not know yet.','It assumes they already know every library rule.','It tells them to buy their own library.','It describes a character’s dream.','First time identifies the audience; the location of the return slot supplies useful orientation.')
q('Message: Coach, I cannot attend training on Tuesday because I have a school performance. I will return on Thursday.','What is the writer mainly telling the coach?','Their availability for training.','Why everyone should stop playing sport.','How to organise a theatre company.','An imaginary adventure from a story.','The dates and reason explain one participant’s absence and planned return.')
q('Recipe step: Stir the oats and milk in the bowl until they are evenly mixed.','What reader action does this step guide?','Combining ingredients while preparing food.','Choosing a character for a play.','Finding the price of a bowl.','Voting for the best breakfast cereal.','The action verb stir, named ingredients and mixing condition guide a preparation step.')
q('Display note: The left drawing shows our garden in autumn. The right drawing shows the same place in spring.','How does the note help a visitor?','It explains how to interpret the two drawings.','It asks the visitor to plant a forest.','It tells a fictional story about a drawing that talks.','It explains how to pay for the drawings.','Left, right and the two seasons identify what each drawing represents.')
q('Email: Dear volunteers, please arrive at 8 am to arrange the chairs before visitors enter at 9 am.','Which clue identifies the intended readers?','The greeting “Dear volunteers” and their setup job.','The message contains two numbers.','The word visitors means every visitor must arrange chairs.','The email must be for children because it is short.','The greeting and role-specific task identify helpers preparing the event, not all later visitors.')
q('Information card: In this game, a draw means both teams finish with the same score.','Why would this sentence help someone new to the game?','It defines a term they may not know.','It invites them to buy drawing pencils.','It promises their team will win.','It tells them which team is playing tomorrow.','The sentence explicitly explains the game meaning of draw; it does not use the art meaning.')
q('Lost-property notice: A blue hat with a yellow button was found beside the oval. Ask at the office if it is yours.','What is the main practical purpose?','To help the owner recover the hat.','To persuade everyone to wear blue hats.','To entertain readers with a hat’s adventures.','To explain how buttons are manufactured.','The description helps the owner recognise the hat and the office instruction gives a collection path.')
q('For teachers: This pack contains enough activity cards for six groups. Each group needs one pair of scissors.','Who would use these details to prepare the activity?','Teachers organising group materials.','Passengers finding a bus stop.','Characters inside an adventure story.','People choosing a hat to buy.','Group quantities and equipment needs help a teacher set up a class activity.')
q('Diary entry: On Monday our class visited the gallery. We sketched one sculpture and asked the guide about its materials.','What does this entry mainly recount?','Things the class did during a visit.','Reasons everyone must pay for a gallery membership.','Steps for making every kind of sculpture.','A made-up conversation between sculptures.','The entry records past actions from a visit rather than giving a sales argument or full procedure.')
q('Map key: A dotted line marks a walking path. A blue circle marks a drinking fountain.','What is the writer helping map users do?','Understand the map’s symbols.','Choose the prettiest fountain.','Imagine a circle going on holiday.','Build a new walking path.','The key matches each symbol with its meaning so readers can interpret the map.')
q('To the families of performers: Costumes should be brought to school in a named bag on Wednesday.','Which part most clearly narrows the audience?','“The families of performers”.','The use of the word Wednesday by itself.','The fact that a bag is mentioned.','The message being one sentence long.','The address names the particular families who need the costume information.')
q('Exhibition caption: Our first tower fell when the base moved. We widened the base before building the second tower.','How do the two sentences inform the reader?','They explain a problem and the change made afterwards.','They guarantee that no tower can ever fall.','They ask the reader to buy blocks.','They tell a story about a tower with feelings.','The caption links the observed fall to a subsequent design change without claiming a universal guarantee.')
q('Class notice: Choose the shaded reading spot! It keeps bright glare off our pages and gives us a comfortable place to share books.','What response does the writer mainly want?','For the class to choose that reading spot.','For readers to count the books already borrowed.','For visitors to learn the history of every tree.','For the class to imagine a talking page.','Choose is a direct appeal, followed by reasons for the proposed location.')
q('Club invitation: Year 3 puzzle fans, join our lunchtime puzzle group. Bring your curiosity and help solve a new challenge each week!','Which readers is the invitation especially trying to attract?','Year 3 children interested in puzzles.','Adults who repair school buildings.','People searching for train timetables.','All people who dislike group activities.','Year 3 puzzle fans names the audience, and join urges them to participate.')
q('Letter: Dear Principal, please keep the old tree beside the oval. Its shade helps us play comfortably on warm days.','How does the second sentence support the request?','It gives a benefit of keeping the tree.','It gives the tree’s exact age.','It tells an imaginary story about shade.','It changes the request to removing the tree.','Shade for comfortable play is a reason offered in support of keeping the tree.')
q('Poster: Bring your empty cereal boxes for our model city. With your help, we can build more streets and towers!','What does “With your help” encourage readers to do?','Contribute boxes to the shared project.','Buy a real city.','Read a finished history of towers.','Take boxes away from the class.','The phrase makes readers part of the proposed project and supports the request to bring boxes.')
q('Book recommendation: Try this mystery if you enjoy solving clues. Each chapter reveals one small piece of the puzzle.','Why is this aimed at a particular kind of reader?','It appeals to people who enjoy clue-based stories.','It assumes every reader wants a recipe.','It says only adults can read chapters.','It names a real police investigation.','If you enjoy solving clues directly identifies the interest the recommendation is addressing.')
q('Appeal: We need volunteers to read with younger children. If you can spare twenty minutes on Friday, please contact the school office.','Who is most likely to respond as the intended audience?','People able to volunteer time at the school.','Only the characters in children’s books.','People looking for a Friday train ticket.','Readers who are being told to buy twenty books.','The writer asks for volunteer time and provides a way for available helpers to respond.')
q('Advertisement: Step into a world of secret tunnels in our new adventure book. Borrow a copy today and discover what is behind the hidden door!','What is its main purpose?','To encourage readers to borrow the adventure book.','To explain how real tunnels are engineered.','To report how many doors are in the library.','To give the complete ending of the story.','Borrow today and discover are promotional invitations; the passage hints at a story rather than telling it.')
q('Speech: Our class should repair the torn games box instead of throwing it away. Most pieces are still useful, and a stronger box would keep them together.','Which words reveal an attempt to influence a decision?','“should repair ... instead of throwing it away”.','“Our class” alone.','The word “pieces” alone.','The number of sentences on the page.','Should and instead of present the writer’s preferred action, with supporting reasons afterwards.')
q('Notice: Garden helpers wanted! Parents and carers, lend a hand with our Saturday planting morning. Your help will make the work easier.','Which audience and purpose fit together?','Adults in the school community; encourage help with planting.','Young readers; tell a magical garden story.','Bus drivers; explain a changed route.','Scientists; report completed measurements.','Parents and carers names the readers; lend a hand asks them to volunteer.')
q('Recommendation: Pick the large-letter signs for our display. Visitors standing at the back will find them easier to read.','What reason is used to persuade?','The signs will be more readable from farther away.','Large letters always contain more accurate facts.','Visitors are forbidden to stand near the display.','Every sign has already been printed.','The argument concerns visibility for the stated viewers, not accuracy or an existing rule.')
q('Fundraising message: Help us replace our broken netball hoop. Every donated coin brings us closer to a hoop everyone can use.','What does the writer ask the audience to support?','Collecting money for a replacement hoop.','Learning the rules of every ball game.','Reading a story about a coin that talks.','Buying a private hoop for one named person.','Help and donated coin support a shared fundraising aim, explicitly for everyone’s use.')
q('Letter: Library committee, please add more books about making things. Many students want projects they can try at home.','Why address the library committee?','They are the group being asked to influence the book collection.','They are the imaginary heroes of the requested books.','They must personally build every project.','They are the only people allowed to read at home.','The request concerns adding books, a decision directed to the library committee; it does not assign them the projects.')
q('Invitation: New to chess? Come to our beginner session. We will explain each piece before you play your first game.','Which phrase makes the invitation suitable for beginners?','“We will explain each piece”.','“First game” means a championship final.','“Come” means only experts may attend.','The word chess makes the audience all sports coaches.','Offering explanations of the pieces meets the needs of people just starting.')
q('Persuasive message: Let’s put a water station near the games area. Players could refill bottles without missing a long part of their break.','Which audience would find this reason most directly relevant?','People deciding how to improve the school games area.','Readers studying imaginary sea creatures.','People booking a flight overseas.','Visitors choosing a bedroom carpet.','The proposed station and its benefit concern how the games area serves its users.')
q('Poster: Give our quiet craft club a try. You can draw, fold or weave at your own pace—no races, no loud music.','Which readers are especially being appealed to?','Children looking for a calm creative activity.','People seeking a loud running competition.','Adults buying musical instruments.','Players learning the rules of netball.','Quiet, at your own pace and no loud music describe the calm experience offered.')
q('Appeal: Please return shared markers with their lids on. Working markers mean the next group can enjoy drawing too.','What makes this more than a bare instruction?','It adds a reason linked to the next group’s use.','It tells a complete fictional story.','It gives the markers’ purchase price.','It names every student in the next group.','The second sentence supports the request by explaining a benefit for other users.')
q('Story: The moon slipped a silver coin into Lani’s pocket. “For the last bus home,” it whispered.','Which feature most strongly shows this is imaginative writing?','The moon acts and speaks like a person.','A pocket is mentioned.','Someone may travel by bus.','The sentence has a full stop.','A speaking moon giving a coin is an invented event, rather than an ordinary factual report.')
q('Story for beginning readers: Pip is a pup. Pip sees a hat. Up goes the hat! “Come back!” says Pip.','Which feature suits beginning readers?','Short sentences and repeated familiar words.','Detailed technical explanations of animal behaviour.','Long lists of historical dates.','Instructions for adults training a dog.','The brief sentences and repeated Pip and hat reduce the amount of new language to follow.')
q('Poem: A spoon went sailing in my soup, / Around a carrot island; / It waved goodbye to one green pea / And steered towards the dry land.','What is the writer mainly offering?','A playful imagined scene for enjoyment.','A factual guide to sailing a full-sized boat.','A warning that soup is unsafe to eat.','A request to donate spoons.','The spoon becomes a sailor in a playful miniature world; no real procedure or appeal is given.')
q('Story: Ari held the empty envelope to the light. One tiny mark appeared in its corner—the same mark as the locked gate.','Which readers might this opening particularly attract?','Readers who enjoy mysteries and clues.','Readers seeking exact instructions for making envelopes.','People needing the gate’s repair price.','People checking a school collection time.','The linked marks and locked gate create a puzzle, a feature suited to mystery readers.')
q('Joke: Why did the pencil take a nap? It was feeling pointless!','What is the main purpose of the question and answer?','To amuse the reader with wordplay.','To explain how sleep changes real pencils.','To persuade readers to buy a pillow.','To report a classroom measurement.','The joke plays on pointless and treats a pencil like a tired person to entertain.')
q('Story: “You can have the first turn,” Mara said, though she had waited all morning. Her little brother’s smile was worth the wait.','Which purpose best fits this extract?','To engage readers in an invented moment between characters.','To list the official rules of turn-taking.','To advertise a new playground.','To instruct every reader to wait all morning.','The extract presents a character choice and reaction as a story moment, not a universal instruction.')
q('Opening: For readers who love make-believe machines: Nori’s umbrella had three buttons—rain, sunshine and somewhere else.','Which feature supports the stated audience?','An impossible umbrella that can change the weather or destination.','A technical explanation of how ordinary umbrellas are made.','A list of shop opening times.','A factual warning about the weather tomorrow.','The unusual buttons promise an invented machine, matching readers interested in make-believe.')
q('Poem: My sock escaped behind the bed, / A fearless woolly rover; / I found it sleeping in a shoe— / Its grand adventure over.','Which clue suggests the poem aims to entertain?','It turns a lost sock into an adventurous character.','It gives a real sock-making procedure.','It asks readers to donate all their shoes.','It reports the measured length of a bed.','A sock escaping and sleeping as a rover is playful person-like invention.')
q('Story: The last lamp flickered. Deep inside the tunnel, something knocked three times. Hana stopped.','What reader experience is this opening trying to create?','Suspense about what is in the tunnel.','Certainty about the ending from a full explanation.','A clear procedure for wiring a lamp.','A sales reason to purchase a tunnel.','The unexplained knocking and Hana’s pause raise a question the story has not answered.')
q('Bedtime verse: Close your eyes, the day is done; / Fold your dreams where stars are spun.','Which audience and use fit the wording?','A child listening to a gentle bedtime verse.','A builder checking roof measurements.','A player reading competition scores.','A shopper comparing torch prices.','Close your eyes and the dream imagery suit a calming verse at bedtime.')
q('Story: Finn reached the finish line last. Then he noticed the younger runner beside him was grinning. “We did it,” she said.','Why is this a story extract rather than a race-results table?','It follows characters’ actions and speech through a moment.','It contains the word finish.','It has no people in it.','It lists every runner’s time and place.','Action, observation and dialogue shape a narrative; a full results list is absent.')
q('Poem: If clouds were pillows, I would keep / A little storm beside my sleep.','What does “If clouds were pillows” invite the reader to do?','Imagine an impossible possibility.','Accept that actual pillows are made of clouds.','Follow a tested method for storing storms.','Buy the exact pillow named in an advertisement.','If introduces a make-believe idea, not a factual claim about pillow materials.')
q('Story for animal-adventure fans: A possum named Patch packed three berries and a map. Tonight she would find the roof where the stars seemed closest.','Which feature matches the stated readers’ interests?','An animal character preparing for an adventure.','An instruction sheet for measuring roof beams.','A report listing wild possum numbers.','A sales offer for three berries.','Packing supplies and setting out with a goal establish an animal adventure.')
q('Comic dialogue: “Your hat is singing,” said Bea. “I know,” said Uncle. “It only knows one tune.”','What makes the dialogue entertaining?','It treats an impossible singing hat as an ordinary problem.','It gives factual instructions for making music.','It states the price of Uncle’s hat.','It asks readers to attend a real concert.','The unlikely singing hat and matter-of-fact reply create a humorous invented exchange.')
q('Story: Under the library rug, Joel found a staircase so small that only a beetle could use it. He fetched a magnifying glass.','What is the writer encouraging the reader to wonder?','Where the tiny staircase might lead.','Which library membership fee to pay.','How many rugs were bought last year.','What time a real library closes.','The unusual discovery opens a story question and Joel’s next action develops curiosity.')
q('Poem: The puddle held a patch of sky / Until my boot went splashing by.','How does this differ from a notice telling children to avoid puddles?','It offers a brief poetic image instead of asking for a behaviour.','It gives a stronger instruction to avoid all water.','It lists rules for school footwear.','It addresses only people selling boots.','The verse describes a reflection and its disturbance; it does not direct the reader to act.')
# Test: fresh texts, not recycled Practice extracts.
q('Message: To next week’s class helpers, watering cans are stored beside the back door. Use one can for each garden bed.','Who most directly needs this information?','The class helpers doing the watering.','Visitors choosing books in the library.','Actors rehearsing a play about rain.','People buying watering cans for a shop.','The address and task instructions identify the helpers responsible for the garden beds.')
q('Report: We rolled each toy car down the same ramp. The red car travelled 80 centimetres and the blue car travelled 65 centimetres.','Which language feature supports an informative purpose?','Specific measurements of what happened.','A request to buy the red car.','A talking car’s dialogue.','A promise that every red car is faster.','The measurements report this observation; they do not establish a claim about every car of either colour.')
q('Invitation: Young artists, join our mural team! Add your ideas to a wall the whole school can enjoy.','What is the main purpose and audience?','Encourage children interested in art to join the mural team.','Explain wall construction to professional builders.','Tell a bedtime story to toddlers.','Report completed mural costs to an accountant.','Young artists names the target group; join and add your ideas invite participation.')
q('Story: The lighthouse blinked twice, then sneezed. “Dusty stars again,” it muttered.','Why is this most likely an imaginative text?','The lighthouse has an impossible human-like voice and sneeze.','A lighthouse can have a light.','The word twice is a number.','Dust can exist in real places.','Speaking and sneezing as a character, not merely the real light, establish the invented scene.')
q('Letter: Dear Council, please add a bench at the bus stop. Older passengers currently have nowhere to sit while they wait.','Which evidence reveals persuasion?','A requested change supported by a reason.','A list of every bus arrival time.','A fictional conversation between benches.','A description of how a bench was built yesterday.','Please add asks for action; the need for seating supplies the supporting reason.')
q('Guide for new players: A “turn” is the part of the game when you make your move. Wait until the person before you has finished.','What makes the guide suitable for its readers?','It explains a basic game term and how to take part.','It assumes knowledge of advanced tournament strategy.','It asks readers to purchase the game immediately.','It tells an imaginary history of a talking game board.','The definition and simple participation instruction support people learning the game.')
q('Poem: My shadow borrowed my new shoes / And raced me down the lane; / But when the sun slipped out of sight, / It gave them back again.','Which purpose is best supported by the language?','To entertain through a playful imagined shadow.','To explain a tested method of lending shoes to shadows.','To persuade readers to buy new shoes.','To give directions to a shoe shop.','The shadow acts like a borrower and racer, making a playful poem rather than a procedure.')
q('Notice: Carers collecting children after chess club should wait at the front office, not the playground gate.','Which detail distinguishes the audience from the children playing chess?','The readers are collecting children after the club.','The notice mentions a gate.','The word chess appears.','The notice uses ordinary sentences.','Collecting children defines the adult pickup role; the location alone does not identify the reader.')
q('Recommendation: Borrow this animal atlas if you enjoy finding where creatures live. Clear maps help you compare their homes.','What response does the writer want?','For interested readers to borrow the atlas.','For all readers to draw every map from memory.','For animals to leave their homes.','For the reader to solve an invented mystery in a tunnel.','Borrow is an invitation supported by a feature likely to appeal to the named interest.')
q('Museum label: This bowl was made from clay. The marks around its edge were pressed in before it dried.','What does the label mainly explain?','The bowl’s material and a detail of how it was made.','Why visitors must buy the bowl.','A story in which the bowl presses its own marks.','The exact name of every previous owner.','Material and process details provide information; ownership history is not supplied.')
q('Story opening: Readers who enjoy gentle animal tales, meet Moss: a little wombat who planted a garden for his sleepy neighbour.','Which feature matches that audience?','A caring animal character and a gentle story idea.','A technical list of all wombat habitats.','A request to donate to a real garden.','A frightening chase with no animal characters.','The caring garden gesture supports the expressly stated interest in gentle animal stories.')
q('Appeal: Save a seat for someone new. A small welcome can make our lunchtime table friendlier.','How does the second sentence support the purpose?','It gives a positive reason for the requested action.','It reports the exact number of seats.','It explains how chairs are manufactured.','It says the table is a speaking character.','The benefit of friendliness supports the appeal to include someone new.')
q('Programme: Families, our class will perform two songs, followed by a short play. The performance ends at 3 pm.','What is its main purpose?','To inform the audience about the programme and finishing time.','To persuade readers that a particular song is the best.','To narrate the complete plot of the play.','To give performers instructions for every dance step.','The order of items and end time orient the audience; no argument or full script is present.')
q('Story: “I ordered one sandwich,” said the giant. The waiter looked at the hill of bread. “That is one,” he replied.','Which feature chiefly aims to amuse?','The humorous difference between ordinary and giant-sized food.','A complete recipe with measured ingredients.','A factual record of a restaurant’s sales.','An instruction to order more sandwiches.','The oversized sandwich and dialogue create an invented comic situation.')
q('Guide: Visitors using a wheelchair can enter the gallery by the ramp beside the east door.','Who especially benefits from this direction?','Visitors needing the accessible entrance.','Only painters hanging work inside.','Readers choosing a fantasy novel.','People looking for a recipe for bread.','The text names wheelchair users and supplies the ramp’s location for access.')
q('Advertisement: Looking for a puzzle that lasts beyond one lunchtime? Try our giant maze booklet, packed with paths to explore.','Which words show the intended reader’s interest?','“Looking for a puzzle”.','“One” proves the reader is one year old.','“Booklet” proves only teachers may read it.','“Paths” means it is only for road builders.','The opening addresses puzzle-seekers; the product invitation then appeals to that interest.')
assert len(rows)==64,len(rows)
items=[]
for i,(text,ask,correct,wrong,reason) in enumerate(rows):
 text=re.sub(r'^(?:Notice|Label|Instructions|Report|Guide|Message|Recipe step|Display note|Email|Information card|Lost-property notice|Diary entry|Map key|Exhibition caption|Class notice|Club invitation|Letter|Poster|Book recommendation|Appeal|Advertisement|Speech|Recommendation|Fundraising message|Invitation|Persuasive message|Story|Poem|Opening|Joke|Comic dialogue|Programme): ', '', text)
 b='practice' if i<48 else 'test';n=i+1 if i<48 else i-47;p=(n-1)%4;opts=wrong[:];opts.insert(p,correct);prompt=text+'\n\n'+ask
 items.append(dict(id=f'AC9E3LY03-{b[0].upper()}-{n:03d}',subject='english',year_level='Year 3',curriculum_code='AC9E3LY03',bank=b,skill='Audience and purpose through language',question=prompt,audio_prompt=prompt,visual={'type':'none'},answers=[{'text':s,'is_correct':j==p} for j,s in enumerate(opts)],correct_index=p,explanation={'summary':reason,'hint':'Use the message’s wording and intended reader response; its topic alone is not enough.'},source={'creator':'SkillrHub original text'}))
Path(__file__).with_name('ac9e3ly03.json').write_text(json.dumps(items,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print('LY03: 64 unique text/task pairs; 48 Practice + 16 Test.')
